WEBVTT

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.279
Thinking Fast and Slow for Label-Free
Open-Catalog Product Retrieval.

00:00:05.999 --> 00:00:08.040
Suppose a shopper picks up one package

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.635
from a shelf of almost identical
products.

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:13.828
How does the store know exactly what to
put on the bill?

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:17.700
In a grocery store with camera-based
checkout,

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.735
a shopper can take an item off the shelf
without scanning a barcode.

00:00:21.735 --> 00:00:23.736
[Camera shutter] A store camera records
the pickup.

00:00:24.076 --> 00:00:28.770
The checkout system has to turn that
picture into a line in a virtual cart.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.247
Each line identifies one product in the
store’s catalog.

00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:37.065
The camera supplies an image, often
blurred or partly hidden by a hand.

00:00:37.065 --> 00:00:41.865
The catalog supplies words: the brand,
the variant and the package size.

00:00:41.865 --> 00:00:46.176
Matching the image to that text is
called multimodal product retrieval.

00:00:47.316 --> 00:00:50.085
Suppose a product comes in a can and a
box.

00:00:50.085 --> 00:00:53.595
Both descriptions carry the same brand
and product name.

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:58.170
The shopper can be charged for the wrong
item if the system confuses the

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:58.687
packages.

00:00:59.027 --> 00:01:01.350
Here is a real GroceryVision frame.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.305
The shopper is holding boxed garbanzo
beans.

00:01:04.305 --> 00:01:08.490
The frozen retriever compares the whole
photograph with the catalog.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.395
The can scores 0.220, the box 0.218.

00:01:13.395 --> 00:01:15.816
The first pass puts a can in the cart.

00:01:16.156 --> 00:01:19.995
A second pass can compare the few
plausible entries more closely.

00:01:19.995 --> 00:01:22.080
That is the job of a reranker.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.884
But how can it learn the packaging of a
brand the store has just added?

00:01:27.024 --> 00:01:30.165
On its first day, a new product may have
a description

00:01:30.165 --> 00:01:31.665
and one catalog picture.

00:01:31.665 --> 00:01:33.810
The store can already search for it,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.115
even though nobody has labeled a
checkout photograph of it.

00:01:37.455 --> 00:01:41.730
As people shop, the camera collects
views of the new package under store

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.500
lighting and in different hands.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.590
These views show what the catalog
picture leaves out.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.593
The store still has no product label for
each frame.

00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:52.050
Why not just fine-tune the retriever?

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:56.415
Labels from brands the store already
sells help it recognize groceries.

00:01:56.415 --> 00:02:00.600
But those labels never show it the new
brand’s box in a shopper’s hand.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.245
ShelfTaught keeps the retriever frozen
and uses the new,

00:02:04.245 --> 00:02:06.435
unlabeled views to train its reranker.

00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:09.496
First we need a likely product label for
each view.

00:02:10.636 --> 00:02:12.345
Figure one has two rows.

00:02:12.345 --> 00:02:14.760
The top follows a pickup through
checkout.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.807
The bottom shows how the slow thinker
learned to recognize the package.

00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:21.150
Start with the pinto beans below.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.435
The teacher compares the frame with ten
descriptions.

00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:28.065
If several candidates remain on the
preliminary shortlist,

00:02:28.065 --> 00:02:30.901
a second question adds their catalog
pictures.

00:02:31.241 --> 00:02:34.980
The four shaded circles are evidence:
two teacher answers,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.440
retrieval scores, and the frame
embedding.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.515
The white circle is what the label model
infers:

00:02:40.515 --> 00:02:42.271
which product is in the frame?

00:02:42.611 --> 00:02:46.410
Option A, refried pinto beans, clears
ninety percent,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.060
and a source named it first.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.325
That gives us one training example.

00:02:50.325 --> 00:02:52.616
Follow its arrow to the slow thinker.

00:02:52.956 --> 00:02:56.565
At the top, a different shopper holds
boxed garbanzo beans.

00:02:56.565 --> 00:02:59.235
The frozen retriever ranks the can
first.

00:02:59.235 --> 00:03:03.555
A single candidate, or too few training
examples for its first choice,

00:03:03.555 --> 00:03:04.967
would end the search here.

00:03:05.307 --> 00:03:09.120
Here three entries remain and the first
has enough examples.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.245
Matching image patches to description
tokens puts the box first,

00:03:13.245 --> 00:03:15.930
with about ninety-two percent
probability.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.573
The teacher stays offline.

00:03:18.713 --> 00:03:21.780
Consider a separate training photograph
of apples.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.450
The frozen retriever ranks Pink Lady
first.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.480
We give a larger vision-language model
the frame

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.807
and the ten most plausible descriptions.

00:03:30.147 --> 00:03:32.145
The teacher favors Royal Gala.

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:35.685
Several candidates remain plausible, so
we ask again,

00:03:35.685 --> 00:03:38.235
this time with their catalog pictures.

00:03:38.235 --> 00:03:40.173
Royal Gala still comes first.

00:03:40.513 --> 00:03:41.955
Both answers are wrong.

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:43.245
This is a Pink Lady apple.

00:03:43.245 --> 00:03:48.075
Taking a majority vote would turn the
teacher’s mistake into a training

00:03:48.075 --> 00:03:48.343
label.

00:03:49.483 --> 00:03:53.775
A label model estimates an unknown label
from several imperfect signals.

00:03:53.775 --> 00:03:55.950
Here it can ask one more question:

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.315
which other store frames look like this
one?

00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:02.955
Each dot is a store frame, positioned by
its similarity to two apple

00:04:02.955 --> 00:04:03.585
clusters.

00:04:03.585 --> 00:04:05.655
The ring marks our photograph.

00:04:05.655 --> 00:04:09.000
During fitting, the model combines these
visual groups

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.270
with the retrieval scores and both
teacher answers.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.657
It also balances assignments across
catalog entries.

00:04:15.997 --> 00:04:18.315
Follow the probability of Pink Lady.

00:04:18.315 --> 00:04:21.330
It passes ninety percent after the first
update

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:25.065
and reaches 99.5 percent after fifteen.

00:04:25.065 --> 00:04:27.435
The retriever also named Pink Lady,

00:04:27.435 --> 00:04:30.205
so the frame qualifies as a training
example.

00:04:31.345 --> 00:04:35.235
Those examples train ShelfTaught’s
reranker, the slow thinker.

00:04:35.235 --> 00:04:39.480
It takes the image patches already
computed by the frozen retriever.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.635
For each patch, it finds the strongest
match among the tokens of a

00:04:43.635 --> 00:04:44.935
candidate description.

00:04:45.275 --> 00:04:48.480
Move to another patch and the best match
can change.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.560
Averaging these best matches gives one
score for the description.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.323
The same calculation scores its
competitors.

00:04:56.463 --> 00:04:58.635
Return to the shopper’s box of beans.

00:04:58.635 --> 00:05:02.475
At checkout, a single candidate can go
straight to the cart.

00:05:02.475 --> 00:05:06.660
The first choice also stands if the
store has too few training examples for

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:07.023
it.

00:05:07.363 --> 00:05:10.995
Here three packages remain and there are
enough examples.

00:05:10.995 --> 00:05:15.975
The slow thinker puts the box first with
91.9 percent probability.

00:05:15.975 --> 00:05:18.795
The cart changes from one can to one
box.

00:05:18.795 --> 00:05:20.824
The offline teacher is finished.

00:05:21.964 --> 00:05:24.750
On GroceryVision, fine-tuning on labels

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:28.320
from previously stocked brands raises
first-choice accuracy

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.165
from 78.7 to 85.2 percent on new brands.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:37.050
ShelfTaught learns from unlabeled views
of the new products

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.607
and reaches 91.2 percent.

00:05:39.947 --> 00:05:43.950
On Klasson, fine-tuning reaches 85.9
percent.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.755
ShelfTaught reaches 93.1.

00:05:46.755 --> 00:05:53.745
On Retail-786k the difference is small:
95.2 and 95.5 percent.

00:05:53.745 --> 00:05:57.589
TransCLIP with teacher answers leads
there at 97.0.

00:05:57.929 --> 00:06:02.010
ShelfTaught updates 340 thousand
parameters in its reranker

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.470
and reuses the store’s image features.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:09.765
Fine-tuning the image tower on the same
training targets updates 63.5

00:06:09.765 --> 00:06:11.865
million adapter parameters.

00:06:11.865 --> 00:06:15.423
On GroceryVision that takes about nine
times as long.

00:06:16.563 --> 00:06:19.575
The shopper leaves the store carrying
the box of beans.

00:06:19.575 --> 00:06:23.517
The checkout system has checked the
pickup against the item in the cart.

00:06:23.857 --> 00:06:26.010
[Phone chimes] Outside, a notification
arrives.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:30.285
In this example the box costs two
dollars and forty-nine cents.

00:06:30.285 --> 00:06:35.057
The receipt shows exactly what was
charged: one box of garbanzo beans.
